@node psf-haproxy-check
@section psf-haproxy-check
HAProxy truncated HTTP/2 PING frame denial of service: checker
@example
HAProxy truncated HTTP/2 PING frame denial of service: checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-110353

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

HAProxy's HTTP/2 multiplexer did not treat a truncated PING frame as a short
read. A PING frame cannot be consumed until it is complete, but its presence
was enough to wake the connection up, so the worker thread never went back to
idle. A remote, unauthenticated client that opens a connection and keeps a
partial PING frame pending can hold a thread at 100% CPU for as long as the
connection stays open (or until "timeout http-request" expires, when one is
set). The impact is moderate and grows with the share of such connections
among all concurrent connections.

AFFECTED RANGE: two sources disagree.
  The CVE description says "3.2 before 3.4.6". HAProxy's own release notes say
  the bug was introduced in 3.1 (the short-read handling was fixed in 2.5 but
  PING was missed, and it only became a problem in 3.1 when the wake-up logic
  changed), and that the fixes shipped on 14 October 2026 in 3.4.7, 3.3.17 and
  3.2.26. 3.4.6 is therefore NOT fixed. This script follows the vendor:
    3.1.x       affected, branch is end of life: move to a supported branch
    3.2.x       affected before 3.2.26
    3.3.x       affected before 3.3.17
    3.4.x       affected before 3.4.7
    3.0 and older   outside this CVE's range (the vendor says 3.1 introduced it)
    3.5-dev     development builds: check the commit yourself
  The same releases fix a second CPU-exhaustion flaw in the HTTP/1 parser (a
  chunked request with a chunk-size line longer than the buffer). The vendor
  does not state its affected range in the release notes, so this script does
  not rule any HTTP-mode HAProxy out because of it.

Vendor workaround (documented for the PING flaw): setting "timeout http-request"
to a low value such as 1 to 5 seconds limits the CPU consumption.

HTTP/2 reachability, which decides exposure: TLS listeners offer HTTP/2 only
when the bind line has "alpn h2". Clear-text HTTP listeners accept the HTTP/2
connection preface by default and upgrade the connection, unless the frontend
has "option disable-h2-upgrade". "proto h2" on a bind line forces HTTP/2.

"check" is read-only; "mitigate" and "rollback" are the only commands that change
anything. "check" finds HAProxy binaries (PATH, usual locations and
running processes), reads the version from "haproxy -v", reads the
configuration files the running process was started with (or the defaults) and
reports HTTP/2 reachability and the http-request timeout. It never reloads or
restarts HAProxy.

Usage:
  ./haproxy-check.sh [command] [PATH-TO-haproxy ...]

  check (default)  read-only. Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = an affected version
                   is exposed or its configuration could not be read.
  mitigate [options]   (root) changes the host. Applies the vendor's workaround
                   for the PING flaw: adds "timeout http-request 5s" to every
                   "defaults" section that has no such line (it never lowers or
                   changes a value you set). It limits CPU use; it does NOT fix
                   the bug, so upgrade as well. The configuration is checked with
                   "haproxy -c" before and after, every file is copied first, and
                   if HAProxy rejects the result the originals are put back at
                   once. Nothing is reloaded unless you add --reload. Options:
                     --timeout SECONDS     value to add, 1 to 60 (default 5)
                     --disable-h2-upgrade  also add "option disable-h2-upgrade"
                                           to those sections, so clear-text
                                           connections cannot be upgraded to
                                           HTTP/2. Leaves "alpn h2" on TLS binds
                                           alone. Breaks only clients that use
                                           HTTP/2 over clear text (h2c).
                     --dry-run             show the changes, change nothing
                     --reload              graceful reload afterwards (systemd)
                     --force               apply even if this version is outside the affected
                                           range (3.1 to 3.4 before the fixed releases)
  rollback [--dry-run] [--reload]   put back every file mitigate changed.
  version          print the script version.

STATE_DIR=/var/lib/psf-security-patches/haproxy holds the backups and manifest.
HAPROXY_CFG (space separated files or directories) replaces configuration discovery.
Containers: with no argument the check also looks in running docker and podman containers
(SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them). A read-only probe captures the daemon's version
output and its configuration (secrets are redacted inside the container before anything is
copied) into a private temporary directory, and this script then runs once per container, in
isolation, against that copy. Paths shown as .../docker_NAME/... are inside the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/haproxy/haproxy-check.sh. Manual page: psf-haproxy-check(8).
@node psf-incus-check
@section psf-incus-check
Incus October 2026 host-compromise flaws: read-only checker
@example
Incus October 2026 host-compromise flaws: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-115375 (instance backup path traversal, CVSS 4.0 9.4)
CVE-2026-115377 (migration stream symlink, CVSS 4.0 9.4)
CVE-2026-115372 (restricted project mkfs argument injection, 8.5)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Incus is a system container and virtual machine manager. All three flaws let a client
the daemon is meant to confine (a user restricted to a project) act as root on the
HOST. A full administrator is already as powerful as host root, so these matter where
you have restricted projects, restricted client certificates, or members of the
unprivileged `incus` unix group, or where an instance can be migrated in from a server
you do not control.

  CVE-2026-115375  7.0.0 before 7.0.2, and 7.1.0 before 7.5.0. Importing an instance
                   backup validates the instance and snapshot names but not the names
                   of the dependent custom volumes in the backup's index.yaml, which
                   become path segments on the host. Any client allowed to create
                   instances in a project can make the daemon create root-owned
                   directories with attacker-controlled content at arbitrary host
                   paths. 6.x is not affected.
  CVE-2026-115377  before 6.0.7, 6.1.0 before 7.0.2, and 7.1.0 before 7.5.0. When
                   receiving an instance or volume by migration (copy, move, refresh,
                   push or pull) through rsync or an optimized btrfs transfer, a
                   malicious source can plant a symlink (rootfs pointing at /) and
                   write through it as root. Any client allowed to create an instance
                   or custom volume, or any server a target is told to copy or move
                   from, can exploit it. Optimized ZFS transfers are NOT affected.
  CVE-2026-115372  7.3.0 before 7.5.0. A user of a restricted project can set
                   block.create_options through volume update, copy and move
                   operations and inject arguments into mkfs, which runs as root.

