@node psf-libheif-oct2026-check
@section psf-libheif-oct2026-check
libheif HEIF/AVIF decoder check
@example
libheif HEIF/AVIF decoder check  (v1)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

A heap buffer overflow in libheif's HEIF decoding (reached through
ImageMagick on an attacker-supplied image) was used to get remote code
execution on a Discourse forum and pivot further. The bug class spans many
libheif CVEs with no single CVE for the Discourse case; the upstream commit
shipped without one. Debian fixed a batch in DSA-6417-1 (libheif
1.19.8-1+deb13u1, trixie, 8 Aug 2026); other distributions backport under
their own version strings, and some stable branches stayed vulnerable for a
while. Upstream security releases have continued (1.21.0, 1.22.0, 1.22.1,
1.23.x). See https://www.hacktron.ai/blog/hacking-openai

This is NOT the Ruby Regexp issue. For Ruby/GitLab, use
ruby/ruby-regexp-oct2026-check.sh instead.

Usage:
  ./libheif-oct2026-check.sh [command]

  check (default)  read-only: every libheif and libde265 on the host, the
                   ImageMagick and libvips that reach them, every Discourse
                   install (whose image-upload path runs an attacker-supplied
                   file through ImageMagick into libheif), and container
                   images, with whether each libheif carries the fixes. For
                   a Discourse container it says whether a rebuild is needed
                   rather than a restart. Root optional. Exit 0 = nothing
                   exposed, 1 = a vulnerable libheif is reachable, 2 =
                   something couldn't be read.
  policy           read-only: whether ImageMagick's policy.xml still lets
                   HEIC/HEIF/AVIF through. Disabling those coders protects the system until
                   you can patch libheif. Exit 0 =
                   HEIF is blocked everywhere a policy was found, 1 = HEIF
                   is still allowed, 2 = no policy found.
  harden           write a managed block into ImageMagick's policy.xml that
                   refuses the HEIC/HEIF/AVIF coders, so a crafted upload
                   never reaches libheif through ImageMagick. Backs the file
                   up first, is reversible with unharden, and is the one
                   thing this script changes. Needs root. This is defense in
                   depth, NOT a substitute for updating libheif.
  unharden         remove that managed block and restore. Needs root. Only
                   touches a block this script wrote.
  mw-harden        block HEIF/HEIC/AVIF uploads in every MediaWiki found, by
                   adding those MIME types to wgMimeTypeExclusions in
                   LocalSettings.php, so a crafted upload is rejected by its
                   detected type before the scaler runs. Backs up first,
                   checks the result still parses as PHP, reversible with
                   mw-unharden. Needs root. Application-level defense; the
                   host libheif still needs updating.
  mw-unharden      remove that MediaWiki block and restore. Needs root.
  hunt             read-only: the journal and logs for image-processor
                   crashes (ImageMagick/libheif/libde265 segfaults, HEIF
                   decoder frames, repeated convert/magick aborts).
                   Exit 0 = clean, 1 = findings, 2 = no journal or log could
                   be read, so nothing was searched.
  version          print the script version.

The real fix is to update libheif (and libde265) and rebuild anything that
bundles it (Discourse containers especially); this script does not run that
for you, because a package upgrade or a container rebuild is an administrator's
call to make and time. The only change it will make is the ImageMagick coder
policy, and only when you ask for it with harden.

Env overrides:
  HEIF_FIXED_UPSTREAM=1.22.0        upstream floor the heap-write class is
                                    fixed at (coarse: distro backports under
                                    older numbers are judged by FIXED_BUILDS
                                    and the package changelog first)
  FIXED_BUILDS="1.19.8-1+deb13u1 1.17.6-1ubuntu4.6"
                                    distro builds known to carry the fixes,
                                    space-separated full versions
  DISCOURSE_ROOT="/var/discourse"   where the Docker launcher lives
  DISCOURSE_APP="/var/www/discourse"  where a bare-metal checkout lives
  POLICY_PATHS="/etc/ImageMagick-6 /etc/ImageMagick-7 ..."
                                    where policy looks for policy.xml
  HUNT_SINCE="2026-07-01"           how far back hunt reads the journal
  HUNT_LOGS="/var/log/nginx/*.log"  extra log globs for hunt
  BACKUP_ROOT=/var/backups/libheif-oct2026
                                    where harden and mw-harden keep the backup
                                    of each config file they change
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/heif/libheif-oct2026-check.sh. Manual page: psf-libheif-oct2026-check(8).
@node psf-linux-kernel-check
@section psf-linux-kernel-check
Linux kernel ebtables SNAT out-of-bounds write: read-only checker
@example
Linux kernel ebtables SNAT out-of-bounds write: read-only checker  (v2)
CVE-2026-53266

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

CVE-2026-53266 is an out-of-bounds write in the bridge netfilter ebtables SNAT target.
When the target rewrites the ARP sender hardware address, it writes into a
socket-buffer fragment, which can be backed by a page imported from a file, without
first making that range writable. A local user with CAP_NET_ADMIN who can reach that
path can cause memory corruption, a crash, or privilege escalation to root.

CISA added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 18 September 2026 with
a three-day federal deadline. CVSS 8.8 (NVD, CVSS 3.1); Red Hat scores it 7.5. CWE-787.

  Fixed in the stable kernels:
    5.10.259  5.15.210  6.1.176  6.6.143  6.12.94  6.18.36  7.0.13
  Branches with no upstream fix listed (end of life, for example 5.4): a vendor
  backport is the only fix.

Why this script reads the kernel's origin first. The upstream patch level is in the
release string only on kernels that follow the stable series (vanilla, Arch, Alpine,
most custom builds). Debian puts it in `uname -v`. Ubuntu, RHEL and its derivatives,
SUSE and Amazon Linux backport fixes under release strings that do not say which
fixes they carry (for example 6.8.0-45-generic, 5.14.0-503.el9), so a number from
them cannot be compared with the table above. For those the vendor changelog is the
evidence: the script looks in it for CVE-2026-53266, and points you to the vendor's tracker when
it is absent.

What the exposure lines mean. The CVE needs an ebtables SNAT rule that rewrites the
ARP sender hardware address (the --snat-arp option). Seeing no such rule on the host
does NOT make a kernel safe: a local user with CAP_NET_ADMIN inside a user namespace
may be able to create one in their own network namespace. The script therefore
reports the rules, whether the ebt_snat module is loaded or loadable, and whether
unprivileged user namespaces are allowed, as information for you to weigh. That
reading is the project's.

This script is read-only.

Usage:
  ./linux-kernel-check.sh [check]
  check (default)  Exit 0 = the running kernel has the fix (or a vendor backport),
                   1 = affected, or the vendor backport could not be confirmed.
  version          print the script version.

Env: KERNEL_CHANGELOG   a vendor changelog file to read, plain or .gz
     KERNEL_RELEASE, KERNEL_VERSION_STRING   override uname -r and uname -v
     KERNEL_MODULES_FILE, KERNEL_PROC_SYS    override /proc/modules and /proc/sys
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/linux-kernel/linux-kernel-check.sh. Manual page: psf-linux-kernel-check(8).
@node psf-linux-kernel-lpe-check
@section psf-linux-kernel-lpe-check
Linux kernel local root quartet: checker
@example
Linux kernel local root quartet: checker  (v1)
DirtyAH6 CVE-2026-80844, TUNderflow CVE-2026-81000, PPPoEject CVE-2026-68121,
DiagSpill CVE-2026-74469

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Four local privilege escalation flaws in the Linux kernel, disclosed on the oss-security list
on 18 September 2026 by Asim Manizada, after an embargo agreed with the linux-distros list
(reported to the kernel security team in mid-July). Public proof-of-concept exploits exist.
Each gives an unprivileged local user code execution as root on the systems it targets, and
each can corrupt the host kernel from a container. The bugs are 10 to 21 years old.

  CVE-2026-80844  DirtyAH6    IPsec AH over IPv6 (ah6): ipv6_rearrange_rthdr() does not check
                              that segments_left is within the header, an out-of-bounds
                              access. Needs unprivileged user and network namespaces, or
                              CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW over an attacker-controlled
                              network namespace. Remote crash only if the host routes IPv6
                              and adds an Authentication Header in transport mode.
  CVE-2026-81000  TUNderflow  TUN/TAP: oversized receive headroom underflows a length and
                              leaves skb->data beyond its allocation. Needs unprivileged
                              user and network namespaces, or CAP_NET_ADMIN over one.
  CVE-2026-68121  PPPoEject   PPPoE: pppoe_sendmsg() keeps a pointer into a socket-buffer
                              head that dev_hard_header() can free (use after free). Needs
                              unprivileged user and network namespaces, or CAP_NET_ADMIN.
  CVE-2026-74469  DiagSpill   SCTP: a 16-bit peer transport count wraps to zero, so sctp_diag
                              copies about 8 MiB past the end of its Netlink reply. Needs
                              SCTP and sctp_diag, and NO user namespace or capability.
                              Remote crash only with non-default SCTP options.

  Affected: DirtyAH6 and PPPoEject since 2.6.12, TUNderflow since 4.6, DiagSpill since 4.7,
            through the stable releases below. Every release of a branch not listed in the
            table (5.11 to 5.14, 5.16 to 6.0, 6.2 to 6.5, 6.7 to 6.11, 6.13 to 6.17, 6.19
            to 7.0, and older ones such as 5.4) is affected and has no upstream fix.
  First fixed stable release of each branch, per flaw (DirtyAH6 / TUNderflow / PPPoEject /
  DiagSpill):
    5.10  269 / 270 / 265 / 265      6.12  108 / 109 / 101 / 103
    5.15  220 / 221 / 216 / 216      6.18   49 /  50 /  42 /  44
    6.1   187 / 188 / 183 / 183      7.1    13 /  14 /   6 /   8
    6.6   156 / 157 / 148 / 151      7.2     3 /   4 / not affected / not affected
  The first stable releases with all four fixes: 5.10.270, 5.15.221, 6.1.188, 6.6.157,
  6.12.109, 6.18.50 and 7.2.4.

Why this script reads the kernel's origin first. The upstream patch level is in the release
string only on kernels that follow the stable series (vanilla, Arch, Alpine, most custom
builds). Debian puts it in `uname -v`. Ubuntu, RHEL and its derivatives, SUSE and Amazon Linux
backport fixes under release strings that do not say which fixes they carry, so a number from
them cannot be compared with the table. For those the vendor changelog is the evidence: the
script looks in it for each CVE, and points you to the vendor's tracker when it is absent. A kernel that
is a stable release candidate (-rc) is judged as the release before it.

What the exposure lines mean. They report whether the module each flaw needs is loaded,
built in or loadable, and whether unprivileged user namespaces are allowed. That is
information for you to weigh, not a verdict: a kernel below its fixed release is affected
whatever these show, and a process with the right capabilities, such as a container, does not
need a user namespace for the first three. That reading is the project's.

A container shows the host's kernel, so run this on the host.

GitHub proof-of-concept repositories exist for all four, so there is no indicator to hunt
for in logs and this script has no hunt. It is read-only.

Usage:
  ./linux-kernel-lpe-check.sh [check]
  check (default)  Exit 0 = the running kernel has all four fixes (or vendor backports),
                   1 = affected by at least one, or a vendor backport could not be confirmed.
  mitigate [--userns] [--modules LIST] [--force] [--dry-run]   (root) changes the host.
                   The researcher's protective measures; a patched kernel is the
                   permanent fix: they only act when check finds
                   the kernel affected (--force overrides). Choose what to apply,
                   because each can break software that depends on it:
                     --userns        stops unprivileged user namespaces, using the
                                     mildest switch this kernel has:
                                     kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=0, else
                                     kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1
                                     (restricts, does not fully block). Only with
                                     --force does it fall back to
                                     user.max_user_namespaces=0, which stops ALL new
                                     user namespaces. Breaks rootless containers
                                     (podman, rootless docker), flatpak and browser
                                     sandboxes. The change is applied live and
                                     written to a sysctl.d file. It does not stop
                                     DiagSpill, which needs no namespace.
                     --modules LIST  prevents loading of the listed modules, from:
                                     ah6 tun pppoe sctp sctp_diag ebt_snat (or all).
                                     "install NAME /bin/false" lines in a modprobe.d
                                     file. A module that is loaded now is left alone
                                     (it cannot be unloaded safely here) unless
                                     --force, which still only blocks later loads.
                                     tun is used by OpenVPN, Tailscale and QEMU
                                     networking; sctp by telecom software; pppoe by
                                     DSL dial-up; ah6 by IPsec AH; ebt_snat by
                                     bridge firewalls.
                   --dry-run shows everything and changes nothing. Previous sysctl
                   values are saved so that rollback can put them back.
  rollback [--dry-run]   restore the saved sysctl values and remove the files mitigate wrote.
  version          print the script version.

