openapi-typescript-codegen (npm, through 0.31.0) copies strings from an
OpenAPI document into single-quoted JavaScript literals in the TypeScript it
generates, without escaping them: path keys, parameter names, media types,
response header names, servers[0].url and info.version. A single quote in
one of those values ends the literal and the rest runs as code. A value in
servers[0].url or info.version runs when the generated client is imported;
a path or parameter name runs when the affected method is called.

The victim is the developer or build job that generates or runs a client
from a document someone else can influence. It is not a bug in a server.
The remedy (0.31.0 is the latest release and is affected) is
to stop using the package, or to stop feeding it documents you do not
control. This script finds where it is used and checks generated code. It
changes nothing and never imports, compiles or runs a generated file.

Usage:
  ./openapi-codegen-check.sh [command] [arguments]

  check (default)  find the package in package.json and lockfiles, in
                   installed node_modules copies, and the CI jobs, scripts
                   and Makefiles that run it, flagging inputs fetched from a
                   URL. Root optional. Exit 0 = not found, 1 = found,
                   2 = something couldn't be read.
  scan DIR...      static check of generated TypeScript or JavaScript under
                   each DIR: a spec-derived value that is not exactly one
                   well-formed single-quoted literal is reported.
                   Exit 0 = nothing found, 1 = findings, 2 = bad argument.
  version          print the script version.

Env overrides:
  ROOTS="/home /root /srv /var/www /opt /usr/local"
                        where check looks (default shown). A directory that
                        does not exist is skipped.
  MAX_DEPTH=9           how deep check descends under each root
Containers: with no argument and ROOTS unset, the check also looks in running docker and podman
containers (SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them). A read-only probe copies the
package.json and lockfiles that mention the generator, whole, and the installed
node_modules copy's package.json, into a private temporary directory, and this script runs once
per container in isolation against that copy. Workflows, Makefiles and shell scripts are not read
inside containers. Paths shown as .../docker_NAME/... are inside the container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/openapi/openapi-codegen-check.sh. Manual page: psf-openapi-codegen-check(8).
@node psf-openssl-check
@section psf-openssl-check
OpenSSL DTLS handshake retransmission read (heap disclosure): read-only checker
@example
OpenSSL DTLS handshake retransmission read (heap disclosure): read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-84782

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

OpenSSL's security release of 29 September 2026 fixed 14 flaws. One is rated High:
CVE-2026-84782, "DTLS retransmits handshake messages from a stale buffer offset".
When DTLS retransmits a handshake message it can read from the wrong place in a buffer,
so a remote peer may receive unintended plaintext heap memory in the handshake data, or
the program may crash. CISA scored it 8.2 (confidentiality low, availability high).
Found by Laurent Gaffie (Secorizon); fix by Ryan Hooper. The FIPS module is not affected.

It matters only to software that speaks DTLS (TLS over UDP): VPN servers such as ocserv,
VoIP and WebRTC servers such as Asterisk, FreeSWITCH, Janus and coturn, and IoT
endpoints. Ordinary TLS (HTTPS, SMTP, IMAP, databases) does not use the vulnerable
code. Node.js and Python's ssl module have no DTLS API.

  Affected: 4.0.0 before 4.0.3, 3.6 before 3.6.5, 3.5 before 3.5.9, 3.4 before 3.4.8,
            3.0 before 3.0.23, 1.1.1 before 1.1.1zj, 1.0.2 before 1.0.2zs.
  Fixed:    public releases 4.0.3, 3.6.5, 3.5.9, 3.4.8. The fixes for 3.0 (3.0.23),
            1.1.1 (1.1.1zj) and 1.0.2 (1.0.2zs) are available only to premium-support
            customers, and public support for 3.0 ended on 7 September 2026. Branches
            3.1 to 3.3 are end of life and were not evaluated by OpenSSL.
  Ubuntu:   fixed in openssl 3.5.5-1ubuntu3.6 (26.04), 3.0.13-0ubuntu3.16 (24.04) and
            3.0.2-0ubuntu1.30 (22.04); older Ubuntu releases were still "needs
            evaluation". Other distributions backport under their own version numbers.

How this script decides. A distribution package is judged by its changelog naming
CVE-2026-84782 (a backport), and for Ubuntu also against the fixed package versions
above. A copy that no package owns (a local build, a bundled library) is judged by the
upstream version compared with the table. The script points you to your distribution's advisory when a
distribution package has no changelog entry and no published fixed version to compare.
It also reports DTLS-capable daemons that are running and processes still holding an
old libssl in memory (a library update does not reach a running process until it
restarts).

This script is read-only.

Usage:
  ./openssl-check.sh [check]
  check (default)  Exit 0 = nothing exposed, 1 = an affected or unconfirmed OpenSSL.
  version          print the script version.

Env: OPENSSL_BIN, OPENSSL_PKG_VERSION, OPENSSL_OS_RELEASE, OPENSSL_CHANGELOG, OPENSSL_PROC,
     OPENSSL_NODE override the openssl command, the package version, /etc/os-release, a
     changelog file (plain or .gz), /proc and the node command. OPENSSL_NO_PACKAGE=1 treats
     the installed openssl as not owned by a package (a local build).
     SCAN_CONTAINERS=1 (the default) also reads OpenSSL inside running docker and podman
     containers, judged by each container's own distribution and packages; 0 skips them.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/openssl/openssl-check.sh. Manual page: psf-openssl-check(8).
@node psf-php-session-audit
@section psf-php-session-audit
PHP session audit for the CVE-2026-115560 preconditions
@example
PHP session audit for the CVE-2026-115560 preconditions  (v1)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Read-only. Changes nothing; root is optional but sees more.

On 23 Oct 2026 PHP announced CVE-2026-115560: remote code execution through
session upload progress when three things line up:

  1. session.serialize_handler is php_binary or php_serialize in config,
  2. application code switches it to "php" at runtime before session_start(),
  3. session.upload_progress.enabled is still on.

The fix ships in the next regular PHP release. Until then turning upload
progress off (php2shell-mitigate.sh does) closes it. This script tells you
whether the other two conditions exist on this server, so you know whether
you were exposed and whether anything else depends on the switch:

  * the configured handler and upload-progress setting for every PHP-FPM
    version and pool, and for Apache mod_php, from the files PHP reads
  * per-directory overrides: Apache php_value/php_flag, .htaccess, .user.ini
  * application code that changes the serializer at runtime, through
    ini_set('session.serialize_handler', ...) or session_start([...])
  * pools or SAPIs that share one session.save_path with different
    serializers (a session written in one format and read in another is the
    shape of this bug class even without upload progress)

Usage:
  ./php-session-audit.sh            full report
  WEB_ROOTS="/var/www /srv" ./php-session-audit.sh

Exit: 0 = no precondition found, 1 = all three conditions met somewhere,
      2 = partial match or something worth a look

Env overrides:
  WEB_ROOTS="/var/www /srv/www"   where to look for code and per-dir config
  SCAN_DEPTH=10                   how deep to look
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/php/php-session-audit.sh. Manual page: psf-php-session-audit(8).
@node psf-php2shell-cpanel-mitigate
@section psf-php2shell-cpanel-mitigate
PHP2Shell mitigation for cPanel EasyApache 4
@example
PHP2Shell mitigation for cPanel EasyApache 4  (v1)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619 ("PHP2Shell") are fixed by updating PHP
(8.5.12 / 8.4.27 / 8.3.36 / 8.2.35, or a vendor build that names both CVEs in
its changelog). Nothing here replaces that. This script covers the hosts the
Debian tool (php2shell-mitigate.sh) refuses: cPanel servers running
EasyApache 4 PHP (ea-php74 ... ea-php85) under any handler.

It writes ONE drop-in per ea-php version into that version's php.d directory,
named so it sorts after every other file there and therefore wins over
php.ini and the MultiPHP INI Editor. It never edits php.ini. A file in php.d
is read when the process starts, so the web server and any FPM pools must be
restarted; apply does that unless told not to.

What the drop-in sets:
  session.upload_progress.enabled = 0     every version, patched or not:
                                          CVE-2026-115560 is not fixed by the
                                          21 October releases
  fatal_error_backtraces = 0              PHP 8.5, unpatched builds only
  memory_limit = -1                       only with --memory-unlimited, and
                                          only on unpatched builds (PHP's own
                                          protective measure; it trades the RCE
                                          for denial of service, so pair it
                                          with a request body cap)
  post_max_size / upload_max_filesize     only with --post-max / --upload-max;
                                          capped to the smaller of the current
                                          value and your ceiling, never raised

What check reports, besides the above:
  * the PHP handler of each version (from WHM, when whmapi1 is present)
  * whether each version is patched: by version, or because the package
    changelog names both CVEs
  * settings elsewhere that can undo the drop-in: FPM pool entries,
    .user.ini and .htaccess php_value / php_flag lines
  * Apache RLimitMEM / RLimitCPU, which cap processes Apache itself forks
    (CGI, suEXEC, suPHP) and which PHP's advisory warns against as a memory
    limit, because a failed allocation sends PHP down the vulnerable path
  * web server and FPM processes that started before the drop-in changed

Usage:
  ./php2shell-cpanel-mitigate.sh [command] [options]

  check (default)  read-only report, and the file apply would write
  apply            write the drop-in for every version, verify it, restart
  status           what is in place now, and whether workers need a restart
  restore          remove the drop-ins this script wrote
  cleanup          once a version is patched, reduce its drop-in to the one
                   line CVE-2026-115560 still needs (--force to do it for an
                   unpatched version too)

Options:
  --post-max SIZE       cap post_max_size (for example 32M; never raises it)
  --upload-max SIZE     cap upload_max_filesize (default: same as --post-max)
  --memory-unlimited    memory_limit = -1 on unpatched versions (see above)
  --no-restart          write files only; you restart Apache and FPM
  --no-scan             skip the per-directory scan under the home directories
  --force               with cleanup, also reduce unpatched versions
  -h, --help            this text
  -V, --version, version

Exit: check: 0 = every version patched and nothing to review,
      1 = an unpatched version is present, 2 = patched but something to review.
      Other commands: 0 = done, 1 = failed.

Env overrides:
  EA_ROOT=/opt/cpanel                where the ea-php* directories live
  HOME_ROOT=/home                    where per-directory settings are scanned
  CPANEL_SCRIPTS=/scripts            where restartsrv_httpd lives
  APACHE_CONF_DIRS="/etc/apache2/conf /etc/apache2/conf.d"
  SCAN_DEPTH=12                      how deep the home scan looks

check prints the exact plan before apply makes any change, and restore reverts
a drop-in it wrote. Run check first and review the plan before apply.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/php/php2shell-cpanel-mitigate.sh. Manual page: psf-php2shell-cpanel-mitigate(8).
