wp2shell, WordPress core pre-authentication RCE: checker, hunter and mitigation
@example
wp2shell, WordPress core pre-authentication RCE: checker, hunter and mitigation  (v1)
CVE-2026-63030 (REST batch route confusion)
CVE-2026-60137 (SQL injection in WP_Query author__not_in)

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Disclosed and fixed on 17 July 2026, exploited in the wild from 20 July, and added to
CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 21 July. Chained, the two flaws give an
unauthenticated attacker remote code execution on a default WordPress install, with no
plugins and no login. Observed attacks create rogue administrator accounts and install
webshells.

  CVE-2026-63030  REST API batch endpoint (/wp-json/batch/v1 or /?rest_route=/batch/v1).
                  A malformed sub-request path desynchronizes the matched handlers from
                  the requests, so later sub-requests run under the wrong permission
                  check. Affects 6.9.0 through 6.9.4 and 7.0.0 through 7.0.1.
  CVE-2026-60137  When author__not_in reaches WP_Query as a string rather than an array
                  it skips integer sanitization and is interpolated into the SQL WHERE
                  clause. Affects 6.8.0 through 6.8.5, 6.9.0 through 6.9.4 and 7.0.0
                  through 7.0.1. On 6.8.x the batch flaw is absent, so the SQL injection
                  needs another way to reach it and the full chain does not apply.

  Fixed: 6.8.6, 6.9.5, 7.0.2, and 7.1 beta 2 (every 7.1 release after beta 1).
         WordPress before 6.8.0 is not affected.

  Object cache: when a persistent object cache (Redis, Memcached) is active, the
  SQL injection sink is not reached and the chain does not fire on currently
  affected installs. The site still needs the update; disabling or flushing the
  cache later re-exposes it. Discovered by Adam Kues of Searchlight Cyber.

The fixes are recognizable in the source. The SQL injection fix replaces the is_array
test and implode() in wp-includes/class-wp-query.php with wp_parse_id_list(); the batch
fix adds a guard against starting a new top-level REST cycle while one is already
dispatching, in wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php. A distribution package
can carry both fixes under an older version number, so this script reads the files as
well as the version.

Protective measure when you cannot update at once: refuse both URL forms of the batch
route at the web server, for example in nginx
    location ~* "^/wp-json/batch" @{ return 403; @}
    if ($args ~* "rest_route=(%2F|/)batch") @{ return 403; @}
or in Apache
    RewriteRule ^wp-json/batch - [F,L]
    RewriteCond %@{QUERY_STRING@} rest_route=(%2F|/)batch [NC]
    RewriteRule ^ - [F,L]
Both forms must be covered. The block stops the chain's entry point; it does not fix
the SQL injection, so update anyway. The block editor uses the batch route for some
saves, so expect that editing may be affected until you update.

"check" and "hunt" are read-only; "mitigate" and "rollback" are the only commands that change
anything. It finds WordPress installs by wp-includes/version.php.

Usage:
  ./wordpress-wp2shell-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  find WordPress under DIR (default /var/www /srv /home /opt
                   /usr/share/wordpress /usr/share/nginx /var/lib/wordpress), read each
                   version, read the two source files for the fixes, and look for a
                   web server block on the batch route. Exit 0 = nothing exposed,
                   1 = an install without the fixes, or one whose state is unclear.
  mitigate [options]   (root) changes the host. The documented protective measure: refuses
                   both URL forms of the REST batch route (/wp-json/batch and
                   ?rest_route=/batch) at the web server, which closes the entry point of
                   the unauthenticated chain. It does not fix the SQL injection, so
                   update. Only when an exposed WordPress is found (--force overrides).
                   nginx: an include as the first line of every PHP-serving server block,
                   checked with nginx -t. Apache: psf-wp2shell-batch-block.conf (server
                   scope, so virtual hosts inherit it), checked with a configtest. If the
                   web server rejects the result everything is undone at once. Matches
                   the route anywhere in the path, so a WordPress in a subdirectory is
                   covered. The block editor uses the route for some saves.
                     --web auto|apache|nginx  which web server (default auto)
                     --dir DIR                where to look for WordPress (repeat)
                     --server-name NAME       nginx: only the server block that lists NAME
                     --dry-run, --reload, --force
  rollback [--dry-run] [--reload]   remove what mitigate added and restore the files it edited.
  hunt             look for signs of exploitation: batch route requests and SQL
                   injection strings in web server logs, PHP files in wp-content/
                   uploads, and recent changes to mu-plugins. Exit 0 = nothing found,
                   1 = a strong lead. A clean hunt means none of the published indicators were found.
                   proof of safety.
  version          print the script version.

Env: HUNT_SINCE (YYYY-MM-DD, default 2026-07-17) for file changes;
     WP2S_LOGS (space separated paths or globs) to name web server logs.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/wordpress/wordpress-wp2shell-check.sh. Manual page: psf-wordpress-wp2shell-check(8).
@node psf-wordpress-xss2shell-check
@section psf-wordpress-xss2shell-check
WordPress XSS2Shell pre-auth XSS, October 2026: read-only checker
@example
WordPress XSS2Shell pre-auth XSS, October 2026: read-only checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-64638

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

WordPress Core 4.7 through 7.0.2 reflects the submitted username in the
wp-login.php error message via sprintf() without adequate sanitization. A
parser-differential payload (a whitespace character between '<' and a tag
name, such as "< area") survives PHP strip_tags() because of the space,
then WordPress's KSES sanitizer re-tokenizes it into a live DOM element. An
attacker who can get a logged-in administrator to trigger the XSS can chain
that into full remote code execution via OAuth app-password abuse and plugin
upload. The attack is pre-authentication: no account is required to send the
crafted login form. Disclosed 2026-08-07 by PWN.AI; WordPress shipped the
fix in 7.0.3 the same day.

This script is read-only: it checks the WordPress version, probes for XSS
reflection (a non-destructive POST to wp-login.php that generates only an
authentication failure), audits defensive posture, and searches logs and
plugin directories for signs of exploitation. It does NOT exercise the RCE
chain or upload anything.

Usage:
  ./wordpress-xss2shell-check.sh [command] [arguments]

  check URL (default)  detect WordPress version and probe wp-login.php for
                       XSS reflection. The probe is a URL-encoded POST to the
                       login form: it generates one failed-authentication entry
                       in the auth log and no other side effects. Root not
                       needed. Exit 0 = not vulnerable or not WordPress, 1 =
                       appears vulnerable, 2 = error or unreachable.
  audit URL            all of check, plus: sensitive file exposure
                       (wp-config backups, .git/config, debug.log, xmlrpc.php,
                       .env), security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options,
                       X-Content-Type-Options), and REST API user enumeration.
                       Exit 0 = no critical findings, 1 = critical, 2 = error.
  hunt [WEBROOT]       read-only log and filesystem analysis: web server access
                       logs for POST bursts to wp-login.php, requests to
                       authorize-application.php (OAuth app-password abuse),
                       plugin upload requests, and direct PHP execution in
                       plugin directories; WordPress debug.log for error
                       patterns; and the plugin directories under WEBROOT (or
                       WP_ROOTS) for recently added or unexpected PHP files.
                       Exit 0 = nothing found, 1 = findings.
  version              print the script version.

Env overrides:
  WP_ROOTS="/var/www /srv/www"   where hunt looks for wp-content/ (space-sep)
  HUNT_SINCE="2026-08-07"        earliest date hunt reads from logs
  HUNT_LOGS="/srv/app/log/*.log" extra log globs
  CURL_OPTS="--insecure"         extra curl options (e.g. skip TLS verify)
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/wordpress/wordpress-xss2shell-check.sh. Manual page: psf-wordpress-xss2shell-check(8).
@node psf-yootheme-check
@section psf-yootheme-check
YOOtheme Pro 5 unauthenticated remote code execution: checker
@example
YOOtheme Pro 5 unauthenticated remote code execution: checker  (v1)
CVE-2026-102785

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

YOOtheme Pro is a widely used commercial theme and page builder for WordPress
and Joomla. Its publicly reachable image handler (the route that creates and
caches resized and cropped images) does not fully bind the normalized image
operations and the output destination to the request signature. An
unauthenticated attacker can reuse a signature disclosed in an ordinary public
image URL, inject extra image operations, traverse out of the intended cache
directory, and write an attacker-controlled PNG/PHP polyglot file into an
executable public media directory. That file is then requested to run code.
CVSS 10.0. Disclosed 21 to 22 October 2026. It is not listed as known
exploited, but a CVSS 10 pre-authentication flaw with a published
mechanism should be treated as likely to be exploited quickly.

  Affected: YOOtheme Pro 5.0.0 through 5.0.52
  Fixed:    5.0.53 (released 21 October 2026)
  Not affected: YOOtheme Pro 4 and earlier (vendor statement)

Sources disagree slightly: the CVE record is filed against the Joomla extension
and one aggregator title says "5.0.0-5.0.55". The vendor's own notice, the
changelog and the CVE description text say 5.0.52 and earlier, fixed in 5.0.53,
for YOOtheme Pro 5 in general, so this script applies the rule to the WordPress
theme as well as the Joomla template.

This script is read-only. "check" finds YOOtheme Pro installs and reads their
version. "hunt" looks for what the attack leaves behind: PHP files inside
media, upload and cache directories, PHP code inside image files, and web log
requests that run such files. It sends nothing to your site and exercises no
part of the flaw.

Usage:
  ./yootheme-check.sh [command] [DIR ...]

  check (default)  scan DIR (default /var/www /srv /opt /home) for YOOtheme.
                   Exit 0 = nothing affected, 1 = an affected version found.
  hunt             check, then search for signs of the attack. Exit 0 = nothing
                   found, 1 = an indicator or an affected version was found.
  mitigate [options]   (root) changes the host. PrivacySafe protection
                   documented: refuses PHP files under uploads, media, images and
                   cache paths with a 403, so a file an attacker managed to write
                   cannot run. It does NOT stop the write: update to 5.0.53. It
                   only acts when an affected or unreadable YOOtheme is found
                   (--force overrides). Options:
                     --web auto|apache|nginx  which web server (default auto: every
                                              one found)
                     --dir DIR                where to look for YOOtheme (repeat;
                                              default /var/www /srv /opt /home)
                     --dry-run                show the changes, change nothing
                     --reload                 graceful reload afterwards (systemd)
                     --force                  apply even if no affected install is found
                   Apache: writes psf-yootheme-deny-php.conf (conf-available and
                   a2enconf on Debian, conf.d on RHEL) and checks it with a
                   configtest. nginx: writes a snippet and adds an include as the
                   FIRST line of every server block that serves PHP (the first
                   matching regex location wins), then checks with nginx -t
                   (NGINX_MAIN_CONF=FILE checks a non-default main file). If the
                   web server rejects the result everything is undone at once.
                   The rule applies to EVERY PHP virtual host on the machine, and
                   it also blocks a legitimate PHP file requested directly under a
                   directory named cache, images, media or uploads.
  rollback [--dry-run] [--reload]   remove what mitigate added and restore the files
                   it edited.
  version          print the script version.

Environment: HUNT_WEB_LOGS (extra web access log paths for the hunt). STATE_DIR
(default /var/lib/psf-security-patches/yootheme) holds the backups and manifest.

Version reading: the script reads the standard WordPress theme header
(style.css, Version:) and the Joomla template manifest (templateDetails.xml,
<version>). If it cannot read a version it says so; the YOOtheme dashboard also
shows the version.
Containers: with no argument the check also looks in running docker and podman containers
(SCAN_CONTAINERS=1, the default; 0 skips them). A read-only probe copies only the first few KB of
each theme's style.css or templateDetails.xml, where the version is, into a private temporary
directory, and this script runs once per container in isolation against that copy. The hunt
command does not look inside containers. Paths shown as .../docker_NAME/... are inside the
container NAME.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/yootheme/yootheme-check.sh. Manual page: psf-yootheme-check(8).
@node psf-zammad-check
@section psf-zammad-check
Zammad session hijack and local privilege escalation: read-only checker
@example
Zammad session hijack and local privilege escalation: read-only checker  (v2)
CVE-2026-102489 and CVE-2026-102490

If this software is helpful, consider a donation to our 501c3 nonprofit:
https://privacysafe.foundation

Zammad is an open-source helpdesk and ticket system. The Dutch Institute for
Vulnerability Disclosure (DIVD) reported that two previously unknown flaws in Zammad
were used to breach its own instance, with first access on 21 September 2026. CISA
added both to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2 October 2026.

  CVE-2026-102489  CVSS 9.4 (CVSS 4.0, CISA)  A session hijack that leads to remote
                   code execution as the zammad service user. Exploitable on 6.3.0
                   through 6.5.4. The same code is present in 7.0.0 through 7.1.3 but
                   is reported as not exploitable there. Zammad says exploitation is
                   possible only on 6.5 and older and that 7.0 and later are not
                   affected. 7.2.0 contains hardening for it.
  CVE-2026-102490  CVSS 8.5 to 9.4 depending on who scores it  A local privilege
                   escalation from the zammad user to root. DIVD lists 1.5.0 through
                   7.1.0-alpha. Zammad disputes the report. It needs code already running as
                   the zammad user.

  Chained: CVE-2026-102489 gives code as zammad, then CVE-2026-102490 gives root.
  Zammad's advice is to update to version 7 or take the instance offline.

What this script can and cannot say. It reports the remote chain (CVE-2026-102489)
from the version. It does NOT claim that any version is fixed for CVE-2026-102490:
no source confirms a patch.

This script is read-only.

Usage:
  ./zammad-check.sh [command]
  check (default)  find Zammad, read its version, report. Exit 0 = the remote chain is
                   not open, 1 = exposed or unclear.
  hunt             look for persistence a root compromise would leave: changed
                   authorized_keys, changed root and zammad crontabs, files owned by root
                   under the Zammad root, new SUID files. These are indicators of
                   this campaign. Exit 0 = nothing found, 1 = a lead.
  version          print the script version.

Env: ZAMMAD_ROOT         the Zammad install directory (default /opt/zammad, /usr/share/zammad)
     ZAMMAD_HUNT_SINCE   YYYY-MM-DD for the hunt (default 2026-09-21)
     ZAMMAD_HUNT_HOMES   directories searched for authorized_keys
                         (default /root /home/zammad /opt/zammad)
     ZAMMAD_CRON_DIRS    crontab directories (default /var/spool/cron/crontabs /var/spool/cron)
     SCAN_CONTAINERS=1   (the default) check also reads /opt/zammad/VERSION and
                         /usr/share/zammad/VERSION inside running docker and podman containers;
                         0 skips them. The hunt is host-only.
@end example
The script is /opt/psf-security-patches/zammad/zammad-check.sh. Manual page: psf-zammad-check(8).
